---
import CaseStudy from '../../components/case-study/CaseStudy.astro';
import Section from '../../components/case-study/Section.astro';
import Prose from '../../components/case-study/Prose.astro';
import Gallery from '../../components/case-study/Gallery.astro';
import Brand from '../../components/case-study/Brand.astro';
import Hypebuzz from '../../assets/work/specimens/horizons-hypebuzz.svg';

import hero from '../../assets/work/horizons/hero.png';
import reference1 from '../../assets/work/horizons/reference-1.png';
import reference2 from '../../assets/work/horizons/reference-2.png';
import previous1 from '../../assets/work/horizons/previous-1.png';
import previous2 from '../../assets/work/horizons/previous-2.png';
import aesthetic1 from '../../assets/work/horizons/aesthetic-1.png';
import aesthetic2 from '../../assets/work/horizons/aesthetic-2.png';
import game1 from '../../assets/work/horizons/game-1.png';
import game2 from '../../assets/work/horizons/game-2.png';
import game3 from '../../assets/work/horizons/game-3.png';
import game4 from '../../assets/work/horizons/game-4.png';
import landing from '../../assets/work/horizons/landing.png';
import subevents from '../../assets/work/horizons/subevents.png';
import postcard1 from '../../assets/work/horizons/postcard-1.png';
import postcard2 from '../../assets/work/horizons/postcard-2.png';
import logo from '../../assets/work/horizons/logo.svg?url';
---

<CaseStudy
	title="Hack Club’s Horizons"
	tagline="An identity made up of many identities"
	accent="#fba74d"
	links={[{ label: 'Website', href: 'https://horizons.hackclub.com' }, { label: 'Github', href: 'https://github.com/hackclub/horizons' }]}
	hero={hero}
	heroAlt="The Horizons landing page beside its logo and a printed postcard"
	meta={[
		{ label: 'Role', value: ['Lead Designer', 'Lead Developer'] },
		{ label: 'Collaborators', value: ['Organizing Team', 'Artist'] },
		{ label: 'Shipped', value: ['March 2026', '(Concluded August 2026)'] },
	]}
	fonts="family=Bricolage+Grotesque:opsz@14"
>
	<Section heading="This ain’t yo’ normal hackathon.">
		<Prose>
			<p>
				Horizons is a marriage of two chaotic ideas. We originally wanted to run a very big, all
				inclusive event in the US, as well as run many smaller, but distinct, global events. This
				idea was not easy to explain, and <strong>the burden to communicate it fell upon me</strong>.
			</p>
			<h3>Problems</h3>
			<ul>
				<li>
					We have 6 events with 6 different localizations, aesthetics, and identities. We need an
					umbrella identity that isn’t overpowering, but isn’t <em>boring</em>.
				</li>
				<li>How do we convey this very unique format of a hackathons to a typical teenager?</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="cs-quiet">I also wanted to achieve some side goals with this, specifically making something that feels fun to look at and use!</p>
		</Prose>
	</Section>

	<Section heading="An identity made up of identities.">
		<Prose>
			<p>
				When developing Horizons, I was at a complete loss at where to begin. I started by
				referencing work done by a previous team member.
			</p>
		</Prose>
		<Gallery
			rows={[
				[
					{ src: reference1, alt: 'A retro “RETRO” wordmark exploration by a previous team member' },
					{ src: reference2, alt: 'A retro “Liberation” poster exploration' },
				],
			]}
		/>
		<Prose>
			<p>
				Before they left, they had been working on a highly retro aesthetic, that me and the team
				really liked and appreciated. Unfortunately, while being aesthetic, I didn’t think it had a
				strong appeal. It felt too serious, when the atmosphere we wanted was a lot more relaxed and
				friendlier.
			</p>
			<p class="cs-quiet">It did serve as a good starting foundation.</p>
		</Prose>
		<Gallery
			caption="I continued to reference some previous works, including previous unfinished projects of mine."
			rows={[
				[
					{
						src: previous1,
						alt: 'An unfinished arcade-style project of mine',
						aspect: 541 / 323,
						crop: { width: 100.39, height: 101.24, left: -0.38, top: -0.62 },
					},
					{
						src: previous2,
						alt: 'Another unfinished retro project of mine',
						aspect: 532 / 346,
						crop: { width: 100.14, height: 100, left: -0.06, top: -0.1 },
					},
				],
			]}
		/>
		<Prose>
			<p>
				I started to develop this <strong>ambiguously vintage video game aesthetic</strong>. It wasn’t exactly
				nostalgic, but it definitely took cues from a retro aesthetic and arcades.
			</p>
		</Prose>
		<Gallery
			rows={[
				[
					{ src: aesthetic1, alt: 'An early Horizons game-style screen', aspect: 334 / 198.99 },
					{
						src: aesthetic2,
						alt: 'An early Horizons landing with the checkered horizon',
						aspect: 522 / 311,
						crop: { width: 100, height: 99.9, left: 0, top: 0.12 },
					},
				],
			]}
		/>
		<Prose>
			<p>
				The Horizons aesthetic ended up being really playful, and didn’t feel dominating or
				overpower. I believed that the other events would be able to come up with branding that
				meshed well with our aesthetic. <span class="cs-quiet">But due to timeline constraints, it would take a while before I would know this for sure.</span>
			</p>
			<p>
				We had to settle on a brand, and the team really enjoyed this aesthetic. So we locked in on
				this one.
			</p>
		</Prose>
		<Brand
			palettes={[
				{
					title: 'Primary Colors',
					colors: [
						{ name: 'Aged Beige', hex: '#F3E8D8' },
						{ name: 'It’s Just Black', hex: '#000000', outlined: true },
						{ name: 'Polaroid Yellow', hex: '#FBA74D' },
					],
				},
				{
					title: 'Secondary Colors',
					colors: [
						{ name: 'Flamingo Pink', hex: '#F86D95' },
						{ name: 'Forgotten Blue', hex: '#46467C' },
					],
				},
			]}
			specimenColor="#f3e8d8"
			type={[
				{ role: 'Headings', svg: Hypebuzz, label: 'HYPEBUZZ' },
				{
					role: 'Subheadings & Paragraph',
					family: 'Bricolage Grotesque',
					text: 'Bricolage Grotesque',
					size: 20,
					tracking: -0.22,
				},
			]}
			logos={[{ src: logo, alt: 'The Hack Club Horizons wordmark', background: '#f9f3eb', vector: true }]}
		/>
		<Gallery
			caption="To navigate, you could use your arrow keys and quickly scroll through everything relevant to you."
			rows={[
				[
					{ src: game1, alt: 'The game-style Horizons landing page' },
					{ src: game2, alt: 'The Horizons event picker, navigable with arrow keys' },
				],
				[
					{ src: game3, alt: 'A Horizons event detail screen' },
					{ src: game4, alt: 'The Horizons project submission screen' },
				],
			]}
		/>
		<Prose>
			<p>
				I had <strong>such a blast</strong> working with the game-y aesthetic. I made everything feel almost as if it
				were a game, including the landing and the main functionality. 
			</p>
			<p>
				I definitely did see it more of an artistic and experimental project, and as a result, I
				think I did go overboard in the features I implemented. I would later scale back on this.
				But, I will say it did do a great job at conveying the whimsy behind the program, especially
				at the start.
			</p>
		</Prose>
	</Section>

	<Section heading="Synergy">
		<Prose>
			<p>
				While our events began to finalize, we realized that our game-styled landing wasn’t exactly
				able to convey the many events we had on our roster. It was a largely based on a old source
				design, during when we planned for only one event.
			</p>
			<p>After discussing it with the team, we decided we need a more traditional landing page design.</p>
		</Prose>
		<Gallery
			size="column"
			rows={[
				[
					{
						src: landing,
						alt: 'The top of the redesigned Horizons landing page',
						aspect: 746 / 601,
						crop: { width: 206.32, height: 440.11, left: -2.81, top: -3.48 },
						outlined: true,
					},
				],
			]}
		/>
		<Prose>
			<p>
				I tried to be faithful to some of the game elements of the original design, but I wasn’t
				afraid to get rid of anything that I thought I could not make work. I also incorporated a
				new painterly accent on the black, which I thought would fit nicely. I wanted to subtly
				capture <strong>the feel of a storybook</strong>, since after all, I want people to be making stories at a
				hackathon!
			</p>
		</Prose>
		<Gallery
			caption="The designs of the subevents got finalized too!" rows={[[{ src: subevents, alt: 'The six Horizons subevent identities side by side' }]]} />
		<Gallery
			caption="They got incorporated into their own section!"
			rows={[
				[
					{
						src: landing,
						alt: 'The subevents section of the Horizons landing page',
						aspect: 485 / 277,
						crop: { width: 206.32, height: 620.66, left: -2.81, top: -282.34 },
					},
				],
			]}
		/>
		<Prose>
			<p>
				We did run into some legibility issues with certain events. With better coordination among
				event artists, I think it could’ve been avoidable. I ended up making minor stylistic changes
				to some of the events so they could be better integrated.
			</p>
		</Prose>
		<Gallery
			caption="I also could not realistically exhibit each event’s unique identity in all mediums, including print."
			rows={[
				[
					{ src: postcard1, alt: 'The front of the Horizons postcard' },
					{ src: postcard2, alt: 'The back of the Horizons postcard' },
				],
			]}
		/>
		<Prose>
			<p>
				I do think this was for the best. By presenting Horizons as an overarching event across
				these unique subevents, we help participants visualize how this event hiearchy works. It’s
				understood that <strong>everyone goes through Horizons to get to their specific event</strong>, which is the
				intention.
			</p>
		</Prose>
	</Section>

	<Section heading="Imperfect Identity">
		<Prose>
			<p>
				Horizons as a program ran successfully. With it’s conclusion, I’ve reflected on this concept
				of a brand made up of different brands. I think it’s a concept I’d be interested in
				revisiting, since Horizons has given me good insight on some specific nuances when
				attempting something like this.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>It’s <strong>very possible</strong> to make something not-bland that can synergies with many different things!</li>
				<li>
					You can <strong>never</strong> make something not-bland that will synergies with everything. That isn’t a
					bad thing though! It just means that to add personality to the underlying brand, you need
					to have more thoughtful coordination.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				I don’t think it’s a complete failure here, and I’ve had people appreciate the effort that
				went into making sure each event had a distinct voice.
			</p>
			<p class="cs-pull">But I definitely want to try this again!</p>
		</Prose>
	</Section>
</CaseStudy>
